/* ============================================================================
   6e · THE CIRCUITS WING
   Every experiment is the same bench — a schematic you can edit, with an
   instrument underneath — loaded with a different circuit and pointed at a
   different instrument.
   ============================================================================ */

const CIRCUIT_GROUPS = [
{ g:'Build it yourself', items:[
  {n:'Circuit sandbox — build anything, run it in time', ex:'R · C · L · transformers · op amps · diodes · switches', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{},
   out:'A driven series RLC to start with. Pick a part, click the board to place it, click pin to pin to wire it, and press Run. The oscilloscope underneath plots whatever you tick; the panel checks Kirchhoff\'s current law and Tellegen\'s theorem against the solution on every frame.',
   note:'Everything here is solved by <b>modified nodal analysis</b> in real SI units. One equation per node says <b>Σ i = 0</b>; every branch whose current cannot be written from the node voltages alone — an inductor, a voltage source, a transformer winding, an op-amp output — contributes one extra unknown and one extra equation. Capacitors and inductors enter through <b>trapezoidal companion models</b>, so an LC tank neither gains nor loses energy no matter how long you leave it running. Try the seven instruments along the top of the panel: the same circuit seen as a waveform, a Bode plot, a DC sweep, a spectrum, a power budget, a rotating phasor diagram.'},
  {n:'Write the circuit down instead of drawing it', ex:'V1 in 0 SIN(5 1k) · R1 in out 1k · C1 out 0 100n', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ net:'* an RC low-pass, written out\nV1 in 0 SIN(5 1k)\nR1 in out 1k\nC1 out 0 100n',
          pane:'bode', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Four lines of text, laid out and solved. Every node in the netlist has become a vertical rail, every part a row of its own, and the Bode plot underneath is the response of the circuit those words describe. Edit the text in the panel and press <b>Build it</b>; press <b>Read the board back</b> to see any circuit you have drawn printed in the same form.',
   note:'A <b>netlist</b> is how circuits are actually exchanged — it is what a schematic editor exports, what SPICE has read since 1973, and what four lines of a message can carry when a drawing cannot. The format is <b>name  node  node  value</b>, with the first letter of the name choosing the part, and node names are whatever words you find clearest. What happens next is the interesting part: the bench is <i>geometric</i>, and recovers connectivity from where things touch on the board, so the text has to be <b>placed</b> before it can be solved. Each node becomes a vertical rail, each part a row, and each part is set down at a column where neither it nor its pins can land on a rail by accident — so a lead crossing three rails it does not belong to is drawn crossing them, and crossing is not connection, exactly as on paper. Get that placement wrong and you do not get a crooked picture, you get a different circuit; there is a ten-resistor chain in the unit suite whose current would be wrong by 11% if any lead ever shorted a rail it crossed.'},
  {n:'Ohm\'s law and the voltage divider', ex:'v = i R, and why the midpoint sits where it does', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'dc', val:10 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'R',name:'R2',val:1000}]),
          pane:'sweep', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true }, sweep:{ name:'V1', from:0, to:10, probe:2 } },
   out:'Two equal resistors across 10 V. The midpoint reads exactly 5 V, the same 5 mA flows through both — series elements have no choice about that — and the DC sweep underneath shows the output is a straight line through the origin: the divider is linear.',
   note:'The divider ratio <b>v_out = v_in · R₂/(R₁+R₂)</b> falls straight out of two facts: the same current <b>i</b> passes through both resistors, and <b>v = iR</b> for each. Change R₂ to 4 kΩ and the midpoint moves to 8 V. Notice what the current does <i>not</i> do: it is not "used up" by R₁. The same 5 mA that enters R₁ leaves R₂ — what the resistors consume is <i>energy</i>, at a rate <b>p = i²R</b>, not charge. Watch the carriers: they move at the same speed everywhere in the loop.'},
  {n:'Two probes, any circuit — the dynamic solver', ex:'ΔV, Thévenin R_th, and the current if you shorted them', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibWheatstone({}), pane:'none', probe:[-4,0], probeB:[2,0],
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A Wheatstone bridge with one arm 10% high. Probe A sits on the left midpoint and probe B on the right, and the panel reports the difference between them — about 119 mV — together with the Thévenin resistance looking back into those two points and the current that would flow if you shorted them.',
   note:'This works on <b>any</b> circuit you build, not just this one. Pick the <b>probe A</b> or <b>probe B</b> tool and click any wire or pin; drag them around and watch the numbers follow. Three quantities come back. <b>ΔV</b> is simply the difference of two solved node potentials, because an ideal voltmeter draws nothing. <b>Z_th</b> is found the way you would find it on a bench — switch every independent source off, push 1 A in at one probe and out at the other, and see what voltage appears; that is the definition of the driving-point impedance. Then <b>Thévenin\'s theorem</b> says the entire network, however large, is indistinguishable from a single source <b>V_th = ΔV</b> behind a single impedance <b>Z_th</b> — so the short-circuit current is <b>V_th/Z_th</b> and the most power any load could draw is <b>V_th²/4R_th</b>. Balance the bridge by setting R_d back to 1 kΩ and ΔV drops to exactly zero while R_th stays put.'},
  {n:'Kirchhoff\'s laws in a two-loop network', ex:'Σi = 0 at every node, Σv = 0 round every loop', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibTwoLoop({}), pane:'power', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A ladder with two loops. At the junction the incoming current splits in inverse proportion to the two paths\' resistance, and the panel reports the largest current mismatch at any node — it is zero to machine precision, because that is the equation being solved.',
   note:'<b>KCL</b> is charge conservation: charge does not pile up at a junction, so <b>Σ i = 0</b> there. <b>KVL</b> is energy conservation: potential is a single-valued function of position, so going round any loop and back returns you to the same value, <b>Σ v = 0</b>. Nodal analysis writes KCL at every node and gets KVL for free, because working with node <i>potentials</i> makes every loop sum identically zero. The power bars below show where the energy actually goes: the source bar is negative (it delivers) and the resistor bars are positive (they absorb), and the two sums match exactly — <b>Tellegen\'s theorem</b>.'}
]},
{ g:'Series and parallel', items:[
  {n:'Resistors in series and in parallel', ex:'R_s = R₁+R₂ · 1/R_p = 1/R₁+1/R₂', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'dc', val:8 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'R',name:'R2',val:3000}]),
          pane:'power', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'In series the same 2 mA passes through both — it has nowhere else to go — and the 8 V divides in proportion to resistance: 2 V across the 1 kΩ, 6 V across the 3 kΩ. The pair behaves as one 4 kΩ resistor.',
   note:'Two rules, and both follow from Kirchhoff alone. <b>In series the current is shared and the voltages add</b>: <b>v = iR₁ + iR₂ = i(R₁+R₂)</b>, so <b>R_s = R₁ + R₂</b>. <b>In parallel the voltage is shared and the currents add</b>: <b>i = v/R₁ + v/R₂</b>, so <b>1/R_p = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂</b>, often written <b>R_p = R₁R₂/(R₁+R₂)</b>. Notice the asymmetry that trips everyone up: series resistance is dominated by the <i>largest</i> element, parallel resistance by the <i>smallest</i>. A 1 Ω in parallel with a 1 MΩ is 0.999999 Ω — the big one may as well not be there. The power bars show the 3 kΩ dissipating three times as much, since with equal current <b>p = i²R</b>.'},
  {n:'The same two, in parallel', ex:'same voltage, currents add', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibParallel('R', { src:{ wave:'dc', val:8 }, a:{ val:1000 }, b:{ val:3000 } }),
          pane:'power', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Now both resistors see the whole 8 V, and the currents add: 8 mA through the 1 kΩ and 2.67 mA through the 3 kΩ, 10.67 mA in total. The pair behaves as one 750 Ω resistor — smaller than either.',
   note:'Watch the carriers: they move three times faster through the 1 kΩ branch, because current divides <i>inversely</i> with resistance — <b>i₁/i₂ = R₂/R₁</b>. That is the current-divider rule, and it is the exact dual of the voltage divider you met in series. Adding a parallel path can only ever <i>increase</i> total current, so parallel resistance always falls: giving charge another route cannot make it harder to travel. This is why every extra appliance you plug in draws more from the supply rather than less.'},
  {n:'Capacitors and inductors combine the other way round', ex:'C adds in parallel, L adds in series', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibParallel('C', { src:{ wave:'sin', amp:5, freq:500 }, a:{ val:1e-6 }, b:{ val:2e-6 } }),
          pane:'scope', tscale:2e-2, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Two capacitors in parallel across the same source. The 2 µF draws exactly twice the current of the 1 µF, so together they behave as a single 3 µF — capacitance in parallel simply adds.',
   note:'A capacitor in parallel is <b>more plate area</b>, so <b>C_p = C₁ + C₂</b>; in series the plates are further apart and the charge must cross both, so <b>1/C_s = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂</b>. That is the exact opposite of resistors, and the reason is impedance: <b>Z_C = 1/(jωC)</b> has C on the bottom, so a rule that adds impedances in series adds <i>reciprocal</i> capacitances. Inductors go the other way again — <b>Z_L = jωL</b> puts L on top, so inductors add in series like resistors, <b>L_s = L₁ + L₂</b>, and combine reciprocally in parallel. One rule underneath all six: <b>impedances add in series and add reciprocally in parallel</b>, always.'}
]},
{ g:'The three passives, one at a time', items:[
  {n:'The capacitor: i = C dv/dt', ex:'charging through a resistor — the exponential', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'dc', val:5 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-6,ic:0}]),
          uic:true, pane:'scope', tscale:2e-3 },
   out:'The capacitor voltage climbs as v(t) = V(1 − e^(−t/RC)) and the current falls as i(t) = (V/R)e^(−t/RC). At one time constant τ = RC = 1 ms it has reached 63.2% of the way; the panel prints τ and you can read the crossing off the trace.',
   note:'A capacitor is two conductors with a field between them, and its defining relation is <b>i = C dv/dt</b>: current flows only while the voltage is <i>changing</i>. That is why it looks like a short circuit the instant you connect it (dv/dt is enormous) and like an open circuit once it is charged (dv/dt = 0). The energy it holds is <b>U = ½Cv²</b> and it is stored in the electric field between the plates — switch on the <b>potential map</b> and <b>electric field E</b> to see that field, fringing round the plate edges exactly as the Laplace solution says it must.'},
  {n:'The inductor: v = L di/dt', ex:'the same exponential, with the roles swapped', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'dc', val:5 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'L',name:'L1',val:1,ic:0}]),
          uic:true, pane:'scope', tscale:2e-3 },
   out:'Now it is the current that climbs exponentially, i(t) = (V/R)(1 − e^(−tR/L)), with τ = L/R. The inductor voltage starts at the full 5 V and decays to zero — the mirror image of the capacitor.',
   note:'An inductor opposes a <i>change</i> of current: <b>v = L di/dt</b>. At the instant of switch-on the current is still zero and the inductor holds off the entire supply voltage; long afterwards the current is steady, di/dt = 0, and the inductor is just a piece of wire. Its energy <b>U = ½Li²</b> lives in the magnetic field threading the coil. The duality with the capacitor is exact — swap v ↔ i and C ↔ L and each equation becomes the other, which is why the two curves here are each other\'s reflection.'},
  {n:'Reactance: why C and L care about frequency', ex:'X_C = 1/ωC falls, X_L = ωL rises', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:1, freq:1000 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-7}]),
          pane:'bode', bode:{ f0:10, f1:1e6, n:170, probe:2 } },
   out:'The same RC network swept from 10 Hz to 1 MHz. Below the corner the capacitor is effectively open and the output follows the input; above it the reactance X_C = 1/(2πfC) falls, and the response rolls off at exactly 20 dB per decade with the phase heading to −90°.',
   note:'For a sinusoid, differentiation becomes multiplication by <b>jω</b>, so <b>i = C dv/dt</b> becomes <b>i = jωC·v</b> and the capacitor acquires an <i>impedance</i> <b>Z_C = 1/(jωC) = −j/(ωC)</b>. The j says the current leads the voltage by a quarter cycle; the 1/ω says it conducts better the faster you shake it. An inductor is the other way: <b>Z_L = jωL</b>. Everything you know about resistor networks then carries over unchanged, with complex numbers doing the phase bookkeeping — that is the whole content of AC circuit theory. The −3 dB line marks the corner <b>f_c = 1/(2πRC)</b>.'},
  {n:'The same network as a high-pass', ex:'take the output across the resistor instead', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:1, freq:1000 }, [{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-7},{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000}]),
          pane:'bode', bode:{ f0:10, f1:1e6, n:170, probe:2 } },
   out:'Identical parts, identical corner frequency — but the response is now the mirror image, rising at 20 dB per decade and flattening above f_c, with the phase running from +90° down to zero.',
   note:'Nothing about the components changed; only where you chose to measure. The capacitor and resistor share the same current, so their voltages always add up to the input, and <b>H_low(jω) + H_high(jω) = 1</b> exactly. A filter is not a property of a part — it is a property of a part <i>and a choice of output</i>. This is worth remembering whenever a datasheet says "RC filter" without saying which node.'}
]},
{ g:'Resonance', items:[
  {n:'The LC circuit — energy with nowhere to go', ex:'½Cv² ⇄ ½Li², forever', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibLC({ c:{ val:1e-6, ic:5 }, l:{ val:1e-3 } }), uic:true,
          pane:'scope', tscale:5e-3, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A charged capacitor and an inductor, and nothing else — no resistance anywhere. The energy sloshes between the electric field of the capacitor and the magnetic field of the inductor at f₀ = 5.03 kHz, and because there is nowhere for it to go, it never stops. The panel shows the total stored energy holding constant.',
   note:'With no resistor, KVL gives <b>L d²q/dt² + q/C = 0</b> — simple harmonic motion, with solution <b>q(t) = q₀cos(ω₀t)</b> and <b>ω₀ = 1/√(LC)</b>. The capacitor starts holding all the energy as <b>½Cv²</b>; a quarter cycle later the current is at its peak and the inductor holds all of it as <b>½Li²</b>. Their sum is constant, which the readout verifies to a fraction of a percent even after many cycles — a real test of the integrator, because backward Euler would visibly damp this and produce a lie. The mechanical analogue is a frictionless mass on a spring, and the mathematics is not merely similar but identical.'},
  {n:'Series RLC — ringing, and the three regimes', ex:'ζ = (R/2)√(C/L) decides everything', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'square', amp:1, freq:200, tr:0.002 },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:10},{kind:'L',name:'L1',val:1e-3},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-6}]),
          pane:'scope', tscale:2e-2 },
   out:'A square wave kicks the circuit at every edge and it rings at f_d = f₀√(1−ζ²) ≈ 4.97 kHz, decaying as e^(−ζω₀t). The panel prints ω₀, ζ and Q; raise R1 towards 63 Ω and the ringing disappears at ζ = 1.',
   note:'This is the electrical harmonic oscillator, and the mathematics is identical to a mass on a spring: <b>L d²q/dt² + R dq/dt + q/C = v(t)</b>, with inductance playing mass, resistance playing friction, and 1/C playing stiffness. The undamped frequency is <b>ω₀ = 1/√(LC)</b>; the damping ratio <b>ζ = (R/2)√(C/L)</b> sorts the behaviour into three cases — <b>ζ &lt; 1</b> underdamped and ringing, <b>ζ = 1</b> critically damped and quickest without overshoot, <b>ζ &gt; 1</b> overdamped and sluggish. Sweep R1 and watch the three regimes in turn. Every second-order system you will ever meet behaves this way.'},
  {n:'Resonance: the peak, Q and bandwidth', ex:'at ω₀ the reactances cancel and Z = R', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:1, freq:5033 },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:50},{kind:'L',name:'L1',val:1e-3},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-6}]),
          pane:'bode', drive:'V1', bode:{ f0:100, f1:3e5, n:200, probe:'V1' } },
   out:'The current through the loop, swept in frequency. It peaks sharply at f₀ = 5.033 kHz where the inductive and capacitive reactances cancel exactly and the impedance collapses to the bare 50 Ω — the readout confirms the reactance is zero there to eleven digits.',
   note:'At resonance <b>ωL = 1/ωC</b>, so <b>Z = R + j(ωL − 1/ωC) = R</b>: the circuit forgets it contains any reactance at all. The sharpness is the <b>quality factor Q = ω₀L/R = 1/2ζ</b>, and the −3 dB width of the peak is <b>Δf = f₀/Q</b> — a high-Q circuit is a narrow filter and a long ringer, which are the same statement in two domains. Drop R1 to 5 Ω and watch the peak grow ten times sharper. This is how a radio picks one station out of the thousands arriving at its antenna.'},
  {n:'The parallel tank — resonance the other way up', ex:'a current source into R ∥ L ∥ C', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibTank({}), pane:'bode', drive:'I1', bode:{ f0:100, f1:3e5, n:200, probe:1 } },
   out:'Driven by a current source, the parallel combination shows the dual behaviour: the impedance is a maximum at f₀, not a minimum, so the voltage peaks there. Below resonance the inductor shorts it out; above, the capacitor does.',
   note:'The parallel tank is the series circuit\'s dual: swap current for voltage, impedance for admittance, and the two problems become the same problem. Here <b>Y = 1/R + j(ωC − 1/ωL)</b> is smallest at ω₀, so the impedance <b>Z = 1/Y</b> is largest, and now <b>Q = R√(C/L) = R/(ω₀L)</b> — larger R means <i>higher</i> Q, the opposite of the series case. Tanks like this set the frequency of oscillators and are the load in every RF amplifier.'}
]},
{ g:'AC steady state, phasors and power', items:[
  {n:'Phasors: amplitude and phase as one arrow', ex:'V̂ = V∠φ, rotating at ω', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:5, freq:1000 },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:100},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-6}]),
          pane:'phasor', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'The phasor diagram on the left rotates at ω = 2πf; the waveforms on the right are its shadows. The capacitor voltage lags the current by exactly 90°, so its arrow sits a quarter turn behind — the phase difference is a fixed geometric angle, not something that changes with time.',
   note:'A sinusoid of fixed frequency is completely described by two numbers, amplitude and phase, so write it as a single complex number <b>V̂ = V e^(jφ)</b> and let <b>v(t) = Re{V̂ e^(jωt)}</b> recover the waveform. Every derivative becomes a factor of jω, every differential equation becomes an algebraic one, and adding two sinusoids becomes adding two arrows. The price is that it only works at one frequency at a time and only in steady state — which is exactly why the oscilloscope view still matters for everything that switches or starts up.'},
  {n:'Real, reactive and apparent power', ex:'p = v·i, and the part that averages to zero', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:10, freq:60 },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:50},{kind:'L',name:'L1',val:0.1}]),
          pane:'power', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A resistive–inductive load at 60 Hz. The bars show instantaneous power: the resistor\'s is always positive, but the inductor\'s swings symmetrically about zero — twice a cycle it takes energy from the source and twice a cycle it gives it back.',
   note:'For <b>v = V cos ωt</b> and <b>i = I cos(ωt − φ)</b> the instantaneous power is <b>p = ½VI[cos φ + cos(2ωt − φ)]</b>. The first term is the <b>real power P = V_rms I_rms cos φ</b>, actually converted to heat; the second averages to nothing and is the energy sloshing in and out of the field. The <b>power factor cos φ</b> is printed in the readout. It matters commercially: a utility must carry the sloshing current through its cables and dissipate <b>i²R</b> on it while selling only the real part — which is why industrial sites are billed for poor power factor and why they bolt capacitor banks across inductive loads to cancel it.'},
  {n:'The transformer — two coils, one flux', ex:'v₂/v₁ = √(L₂/L₁) = N₂/N₁', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibXfmr({ xf:{ l1:1e-2, l2:4e-2, k:0.995, r1:0.5, r2:0.5 } }),
          pane:'scope', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A 10 V primary produces very nearly 20 V on the secondary, because L₂ = 4L₁ means twice the turns. Watch the currents: the secondary carries about half the primary current, so the power going in and the power coming out are the same.',
   note:'The two windings share a magnetic circuit, so a changing current in one induces an EMF in the other: <b>v₁ = L₁ di₁/dt + M di₂/dt</b> and <b>v₂ = M di₁/dt + L₂ di₂/dt</b>, with the mutual inductance <b>M = k√(L₁L₂)</b>. Since inductance goes as the square of the turns, <b>√(L₂/L₁) = N₂/N₁</b>. Voltage multiplies by the turns ratio and current divides by it, so <b>power is conserved</b> — a transformer is a lever, not a source. That is why the grid ships energy at hundreds of kilovolts: the same power at a hundred times the voltage needs a hundredth of the current and loses ten thousand times less to <b>i²R</b> in the cables.'},
  {n:'The ideal transformer, and impedance matching', ex:'a load looks N² times bigger through it', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibXfmr({ kind:'XFMRI', xf:{ ratio:4 }, src:{ wave:'sin', amp:10, freq:1000 },
                                   load:{ val:1000 } }),
          pane:'scope', probe:[-3,1], probeB:[3,1],
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A 4:1 ideal transformer — pure turns ratio, no inductance and no losses. 10 V in gives 2.5 V out and the currents go the other way, so the power in and the power out are identical to machine precision.',
   note:'Strip a transformer down to its defining constraints, <b>v₁ = N v₂</b> and <b>i₂ = −N i₁</b>, and one striking consequence appears. The source sees <b>v₁/i₁ = N²·(v₂/i₂) = N²R_L</b> — the load, magnified by the <i>square</i> of the turns ratio. A 1 kΩ load through a 4:1 transformer looks like 16 kΩ. That is <b>impedance matching</b>, and it matters because maximum power transfer needs source and load impedance to be equal: a 4 Ω loudspeaker cannot be driven efficiently by a high-impedance valve amplifier without one. Put the probes across the primary and the secondary in turn and compare the Thévenin resistance each side reports.'},
  {n:'Coupling k — how much flux is actually shared', ex:'M = k√(L₁L₂), with 0 ≤ k ≤ 1', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibMutual({ k:{ k:0.5 } }), pane:'scope',
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Two inductors that share no wire at all, only flux. With k = 0.5 the second coil sees half the voltage it would if the coupling were perfect. Select K1 and drag the coupling slider from 0 to 1 to watch the induced voltage scale exactly with k.',
   note:'The coupling coefficient is the fraction of one coil\'s flux that actually threads the other, and it can never exceed 1 without violating energy conservation — with <b>|k| &gt; 1</b> the stored energy <b>U = ½L₁i₁² + ½L₂i₂² + M i₁i₂</b> could be made negative, and the circuit would be a source of free energy. Real transformers with a closed iron core reach k &gt; 0.999; two coils sitting near each other on a bench manage perhaps 0.3. Deliberately loose coupling is useful too — it is how a wireless charger, an ignition coil and a tuned RF transformer all work.'}
]},
{ g:'Nonlinear parts: the diode', items:[
  {n:'The diode I–V curve', ex:'I = Iₛ(e^(v/nV_T) − 1)', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'dc', val:1 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:100},{kind:'D',name:'D1'}]),
          pane:'sweep', sweep:{ name:'V1', from:-2, to:1.5, n:120, probe:'V1' } },
   out:'Sweeping the supply from −2 V to +1.5 V draws the exponential itself. Backwards the current is a few femtoamps — nothing; forwards it turns up sharply near 0.6 V and then rises so steeply that the voltage barely moves however hard you push.',
   note:'A junction diode obeys the <b>Shockley equation I = Iₛ(e^(v/nV_T) − 1)</b>, with the thermal voltage <b>V_T = kT/q ≈ 25.85 mV</b> at room temperature. There is no "turn-on voltage" in that formula — the famous 0.6 V is simply where the exponential crosses whatever current you happen to care about, and it shifts by about 60 mV per decade of current. Because the equation is nonlinear, the solver cannot get there in one step: it linearises about a guess and iterates with <b>Newton–Raphson</b>, using the standard junction limiter to stop the exponential overflowing. The panel reports whether it converged.'},
  {n:'The half-wave rectifier', ex:'a diode makes DC out of AC', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:5, freq:200 }, [{kind:'D',name:'D1'},{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000}]),
          pane:'scope', tscale:5e-2, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Only the positive half of each cycle gets through; the negative half is blocked entirely. The output peak is about 4.4 V — the input peak less the diode\'s forward drop — and its average is now positive where the input\'s was zero.',
   note:'This is the first step in every mains power supply. Watch the carriers stop dead for half of every cycle: the diode is a one-way valve for charge. The cost is visible too — the output is lumpy, it wastes half the waveform, and the diode drop is lost as heat at a rate <b>i·v_f</b>. The next demo fixes the first two problems.'},
  {n:'The full-wave bridge, and smoothing', ex:'four diodes and a reservoir capacitor', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibBridge({}), pane:'scope', tscale:5e-2,
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Four diodes route both halves of the cycle the same way through the load, so the output pulses at twice the supply frequency. The reservoir capacitor then holds the voltage up between peaks, leaving only a small ripple.',
   note:'Follow the carriers round: on one half cycle the current takes D1 and D4, on the other D2 and D3, and in both cases it crosses the load in the <i>same</i> direction — that is the whole trick. The capacitor now works as a reservoir: it charges quickly at each peak and discharges slowly into the load between them, so the ripple is roughly <b>ΔV ≈ I/(2f C)</b>. Make C1 ten times smaller and the ripple grows tenfold. Note also that the current comes in short, tall spikes at the peaks rather than smoothly — real rectifiers are hard on the supply for exactly this reason.'},
  {n:'The voltage doubler', ex:'a clamp and a peak detector in series', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibDoubler({}), pane:'scope', tscale:2e-2,
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A 5 V peak sine produces very nearly 10 V of DC — from a circuit with no transformer in it at all. Watch it build up over the first few cycles as the two capacitors charge.',
   note:'Two stages. C1 and D1 form a <b>clamp</b>: D1 conducts on negative peaks and charges C1 to the peak voltage, which then adds itself to the incoming wave, so the node past C1 swings from 0 to <b>2V_p</b> instead of −V_p to +V_p. D2 and C2 then form a <b>peak detector</b> on that doubled swing. Nothing is created for free — the output impedance is high and the ripple is worse than a bridge, so a doubler suits low-current jobs like biasing rather than powering anything. Cascading the same pair over and over gives the <b>Cockcroft–Walton</b> ladder, which is how particle accelerators reached hundreds of kilovolts in 1932 and how the flyback supply in an old television made 25 kV.'},
  {n:'The precision rectifier', ex:'a diode inside the feedback loop', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibPrecisionRect({}), pane:'scope', tscale:5e-2,
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'The input here is only 0.3 V — far too small for an ordinary diode, which would block it entirely. This one rectifies it faithfully, with no forward drop lost at all.',
   note:'Put the diode <i>inside</i> the feedback loop and the op amp is obliged to do whatever it takes to make the feedback voltage match the input. If the diode needs 0.6 V to conduct, the op amp simply drives its output 0.6 V higher — the drop still happens, but it happens inside the loop where the feedback swallows it, and the load sees an ideal diode. The error is divided by the loop gain, so it is microvolts rather than 600 millivolts. Try the same 0.3 V input on the plain half-wave rectifier and nothing comes out. The limitation is speed: when the diode is off the op amp runs open loop and slams into a rail, and it takes real time to come back, so a superdiode fails at high frequency.'},
  {n:'The diode clipper', ex:'two junctions that cut the peaks off', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibClipper({}), pane:'scope', tscale:5e-2,
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A 3 V sine goes in and a flat-topped wave comes out, limited to about ±0.7 V in both directions. Between the limits the diodes are off and the signal passes untouched.',
   note:'Two diodes facing opposite ways, each idle until the signal exceeds its forward drop, then conducting hard and holding the node where it is. The series resistor absorbs the difference, so it is essential — without it the source would simply be short-circuited. Because the transition is exponential rather than sharp the corners are soft, which is exactly why this circuit is the heart of a guitar overdrive pedal: hard clipping sounds harsh, and a diode\'s gradual knee generates a warmer harmonic series. Switch to the <b>Spectrum</b> instrument to see the odd harmonics it creates, and read the THD the panel computes.'}
]},
{ g:'Filters', items:[
  {n:'The RC low-pass', ex:'|H| = 1/√(1+(f/f_c)²), −20 dB/decade', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'square', amp:1, freq:200, tr:0.004 },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1591.549430918953},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-7}]),
          pane:'both', tscale:2e-2, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A square wave at 200 Hz into a filter cornering at 1 kHz. Both domains are on screen at once: on the left the sharp edges are visibly rounded off, and on the right you can see exactly why — the harmonics above the corner have been cut down while the fundamental passed.',
   note:'This is the pairing worth internalising. In <b>time</b> the filter is a differential equation, <b>RC dv_out/dt + v_out = v_in</b>, and its effect is a rounding of every edge with time constant τ = RC. In <b>frequency</b> it is a single complex number per frequency, <b>H(jω) = 1/(1+jωRC)</b>, with magnitude <b>1/√(1+(f/f_c)²)</b> and phase <b>−arctan(f/f_c)</b>. Those are the same statement. An edge is sharp precisely because it contains high harmonics; remove them and it must round. Switch the source to a sine and watch the spectrum collapse to a single line — the filter has not changed, only what you fed it.'},
  {n:'The RC high-pass', ex:'the same parts, the other output', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'square', amp:1, freq:200, tr:0.004 },
                                     [{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-7},{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1591.549430918953}]),
          pane:'both', tscale:2e-2, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Identical components, identical corner — but taking the output across the resistor instead. Now the flat parts of the square wave decay away and only the edges survive, as spikes. The spectrum shows the mirror image: the fundamental cut, the harmonics kept.',
   note:'<b>H(jω) = jωRC/(1+jωRC)</b>, rising at 20 dB/decade and flattening above f_c, with phase running from +90° to 0. A high-pass is a <b>differentiator</b> well below its corner, which is exactly what you are seeing: the flat top of a square wave has zero derivative and vanishes, while each edge has an enormous one and survives as a spike. Note that <b>H_low + H_high = 1</b> exactly, at every frequency — the two outputs of this one network always add back up to the input.'},
  {n:'RL filters — the same job, other parts', ex:'f_c = R/2πL', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:1, freq:1000 },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'L',name:'L1',val:0.159154943}]),
          pane:'bode', drive:'V1', bode:{ f0:10, f1:1e6, n:180, probe:2 } },
   out:'An inductor in place of the capacitor, taking the output across the inductor: a high-pass with its corner at f_c = R/2πL = 1 kHz. Swap which element you measure and it becomes a low-pass, exactly as with RC.',
   note:'Because <b>Z_L = jωL</b> rises with frequency while <b>Z_C = 1/jωC</b> falls, an inductor and a capacitor play opposite roles in the same position — an inductor in series is a low-pass, a capacitor in series is a high-pass. The corner is wherever the reactance equals the resistance: <b>ωL = R</b> gives <b>f_c = R/2πL</b>. In practice RC is preferred at low frequencies because inductors are bulky, lossy and pick up stray fields; switch on the <b>magnetic field B</b> overlay and you can see why the last of those is a real problem.'},
  {n:'Second order — twice the slope', ex:'RLC rolls off at 40 dB/decade', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:1, freq:1000 },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:90},{kind:'L',name:'L1',val:1e-3},
                                      {kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-6}]),
          pane:'bode', drive:'V1', bode:{ f0:100, f1:1e6, n:200, probe:3 } },
   out:'Taking the output across the capacitor of a series RLC gives a second-order low-pass: flat below f₀ = 5.03 kHz, then falling at 40 dB per decade instead of 20 — twice as steep, because there are now two energy-storing elements.',
   note:'Each independent reactive element adds one <b>pole</b>, and each pole contributes 20 dB/decade of rolloff and 90° of phase lag. Here <b>H(jω) = ω₀²/(ω₀² − ω² + 2jζω₀ω)</b>. The damping ζ sets what happens at the corner itself: lower R and a peak appears just below f₀ — resonant gain, useful for selectivity and dangerous for stability. Drop R1 to 20 Ω and watch the peak grow; raise it to 200 Ω and the response becomes gently rounded with no peak at all. Filter design is largely the art of placing poles to get the passband, the rolloff and the step response you can live with.'},
  {n:'The LC band-pass', ex:'a series LC passes only its resonance', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:1, freq:5033 },
                                     [{ kind:'L', name:'L1', val:1e-3 },
                                      { kind:'C', name:'C1', val:1e-6 },
                                      { kind:'R', name:'RL', val:100 }]),
          pane:'bode', drive:'V1', bode:{ f0:100, f1:3e5, n:200, probe:3 } },
   out:'A series LC in the signal path. Far below resonance the capacitor blocks; far above, the inductor blocks; only near f₀ = 5.03 kHz, where the two reactances cancel, does the pair become a piece of wire and let the signal through.',
   note:'The series impedance is <b>Z = j(ωL − 1/ωC)</b>, which vanishes at <b>ω₀ = 1/√(LC)</b> and grows in both directions away from it — so the load sees a peak exactly at resonance, with skirts falling at 20 dB/decade. Its sharpness is <b>Q = ω₀L/R_total</b> and its −3 dB width is <b>Δf = f₀/Q</b>. Lower R_L and the peak narrows. This is the oldest filter in electronics: it is what "tuning" a radio means, and the reason the first receivers were called tuned circuits.'},
  {n:'The notch — a filter that removes one frequency', ex:'a series LC to ground shunts it away', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibNotch({}), pane:'bode', drive:'V1', bode:{ f0:100, f1:3e5, n:220, probe:2 } },
   out:'The same series LC, but placed as a shunt to ground instead of in the signal path — and the response inverts. Everything passes except a deep null right at f₀ = 5.03 kHz, where the LC becomes a short circuit to ground and swallows the signal.',
   note:'Exactly the same component pair, in the other topology: what was a peak becomes a null. At resonance the series LC has near-zero impedance, so it shorts that one frequency to ground while leaving its neighbours alone. Notches like this remove a single interfering tone — 50 or 60 Hz mains hum from an audio path, or a strong carrier from a receiver — and the depth of the null is set by whatever resistance remains in the branch. Give L1 a winding resistance of 5 Ω and watch the null fill in: a perfect notch needs a perfect short, and real components do not have one.'},
  {n:'Filtering, seen in both domains at once', ex:'what a filter does to a shape, and to its spectrum', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'expr', expr:'sign(sin(tau*300*t)) + 0.6*sin(tau*6000*t)' },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-7}]),
          pane:'both', tscale:2e-2, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A 300 Hz square wave with a 6 kHz tone deliberately added on top — audible as a hiss riding on a buzz. The filter corners at 1.6 kHz. On the left the interference visibly disappears from the waveform; on the right its spectral line collapses while the square wave\'s own harmonics survive.',
   note:'This is the whole argument for working in the frequency domain. In the time domain "remove the 6 kHz tone but keep the square wave" is a hard thing even to state, because both are tangled together in every sample. In the frequency domain they are simply at different places, and separating them is a matter of multiplying by a function that is near 1 in one place and near 0 in another. Convolution in time is multiplication in frequency — which is why filtering, an awkward integral in one domain, is elementary in the other. The Fourier wing takes that correspondence apart properly.'}
]},
{ g:'Op amps', items:[
  {n:'The voltage follower — gain of exactly one', ex:'v_out = v_in, and why that is useful', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibFollower({}), pane:'scope',
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'The output copies the input exactly — the two traces lie on top of each other. A gain of one sounds pointless until you look at the current: the input draws essentially nothing while the output drives milliamps into the load.',
   note:'Wire the output straight back to the inverting input and the feedback forces <b>v₋ = v₊ = v_in</b>, so <b>v_out = v_in</b> with <b>β = 1</b> — the maximum possible feedback, and therefore the maximum possible accuracy and bandwidth. What it buys is <b>impedance transformation</b>: input impedance of about 10⁹ Ω, output impedance of <b>R_out/(1+A₀) ≈ 75/200000 ≈ 0.4 mΩ</b>. That is the whole point. A sensor that could not drive a 1 kΩ load without sagging can drive it through a follower without noticing, which is why buffers appear between almost every pair of stages in analogue design. It is also exactly what makes the Sallen–Key filter work.'},
  {n:'The inverting amplifier', ex:'v_out/v_in = −R_f/R_in, from one assumption', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibInvAmp({ src:{ wave:'sin', amp:0.4, freq:500 } }),
          pane:'scope', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'0.4 V in, 4 V out and upside down: a gain of exactly −R_f/R_in = −10. The readout shows the inverting input sitting at a few tens of microvolts — the "virtual earth" is not a fiction, it is what enormous gain plus feedback actually produces.',
   note:'The op amp\'s job is to make its two inputs equal, and negative feedback lets it. If the + input is earthed then the − input must be too, so the input current is fixed at <b>i = v_in/R_in</b>; no current enters the amplifier itself, so all of it continues through R_f, giving <b>v_out = −i R_f = −v_in R_f/R_in</b>. The gain depends only on a ratio of two resistors — not on the op amp\'s own gain, its temperature, or its manufacturer. That is what feedback buys. Select U1 and switch the model from <b>real</b> to <b>ideal</b> to see how little difference 200 000 of open-loop gain makes once it is wrapped in feedback.'},
  {n:'Gain–bandwidth: the trade you cannot avoid', ex:'A · f_BW = GBW, a constant', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibNonInvAmp({}), pane:'bode', drive:'V1', bode:{ f0:100, f1:1e7, n:190, probe:2 } },
   out:'A non-inverting amplifier with a gain of 1 + R_f/R_g = 11. Flat to about 180 kHz, then falling — because the op amp\'s own gain has run out. Multiply gain by bandwidth and you get 2 MHz, which is exactly the open-loop gain (2×10⁵) times its pole frequency (10 Hz).',
   note:'A real op amp is not an infinite amplifier; it has one dominant pole, <b>A(jω) = A₀/(1 + jω/ω_p)</b>, so its gain falls at 20 dB per decade above a few hertz. Feedback trades that surplus gain for accuracy, and what stays constant is the product: <b>closed-loop gain × bandwidth = GBW</b>. Change R_f to 1 kΩ for a gain of 2 and watch the bandwidth grow to a megahertz. The same model carries the <b>slew rate</b> — select U1 and drop it to 10 000 V/s, then drive the input hard, and the output turns into a triangle wave because it physically cannot climb any faster.'},
  {n:'The integrator — an op amp doing calculus', ex:'v_out = −(1/RC)∫v_in dt', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibInvAmp({ fbKind:'C', fb:{ val:1e-7 }, in:{ val:10000 },
                                     src:{ wave:'square', amp:1, freq:500, tr:0.004 } }),
          pane:'scope', tscale:2e-2, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Feed a square wave into an integrator and you get a triangle — because the integral of a constant is a ramp. The slope of each ramp is exactly v_in/RC = 1 V per millisecond, and it reverses the instant the input does.',
   note:'Replace the feedback resistor with a capacitor and the virtual-earth argument runs again: the input current <b>v_in/R</b> has nowhere to go but into C, and a capacitor integrates the current it receives — <b>v = (1/C)∫i dt</b> — so <b>v_out = −(1/RC)∫v_in dt</b>. In the frequency domain that is <b>H(jω) = −1/(jωRC)</b>: gain falling at 20 dB/decade and a constant −90° phase, the signature of an integrator. Real ones need a large resistor across the capacitor, because otherwise the op amp\'s own input offset is integrated too and the output drifts into a rail within seconds. Analogue computers were rooms full of these, solving differential equations in real time by wiring up the equation itself.'},
  {n:'The differentiator — the same trick, reversed', ex:'v_out = −RC dv_in/dt', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibInvAmp({ inKind:'C', in:{ val:1e-7 }, fb:{ val:10000 },
                                     src:{ wave:'tri', amp:1, freq:500 } }),
          pane:'scope', tscale:2e-2, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Swap the two components over and the operation inverts: a triangle wave in gives a square wave out, because the derivative of a straight ramp is a constant that flips sign at every corner.',
   note:'Now the capacitor is the input element, so the current it passes is <b>C dv_in/dt</b>, and that current through R_f gives <b>v_out = −R_f C dv_in/dt</b>. In the frequency domain <b>H(jω) = −jωR_fC</b> — gain <i>rising</i> at 20 dB/decade, phase +90°. That rising gain is the practical problem: a differentiator amplifies high frequencies without limit, so it amplifies noise without limit too, and combined with the op amp\'s own falling gain it readily oscillates. Real designs add a small resistor in series with the capacitor to stop the gain rising past a chosen frequency. Integrators are well behaved and differentiators are trouble, which is why analogue computers were built almost entirely from the former.'},
  {n:'The summing amplifier — an analogue adder', ex:'v_out = −R_f(v₁/R₁ + v₂/R₂)', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibSummer({}), pane:'scope', tscale:2e-2,
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A steady 1 V and a 0.5 V sine arrive through separate resistors and meet at the virtual earth. The output is their inverted sum — the sine, shifted down by the DC term and turned upside down.',
   note:'This is the virtual-earth argument paying off. Because the inverting input is held at 0 V, each input drives its own current <b>v_k/R_k</b> into that node <i>without knowing the others exist</i> — the inputs cannot interact, because there is no voltage at the summing junction for them to interact through. All those currents meet and continue through R_f, giving <b>v_out = −R_f(v₁/R₁ + v₂/R₂ + …)</b>. Equal resistors give a plain sum; unequal ones give a <b>weighted</b> sum, which is how an R-2R ladder turns bits into a voltage inside every digital-to-analogue converter, and how analogue audio mixers work. Change R_b to 5 kΩ and the sine contributes twice as much.'},
  {n:'The difference amplifier — an analogue subtractor', ex:'v_out = (R_f/R₁)(v₂ − v₁)', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibDifference({}), pane:'power',
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'5 V on one input, 3 V on the other, matched resistor pairs, and an output of exactly +2 V. Raise both inputs together by any amount and the output does not move at all — it responds only to the difference.',
   note:'Superposition does this in two lines. Kill v₂ and the circuit is an inverting amplifier: <b>−v₁R_f/R₁</b>. Kill v₁ and it is a divider into a non-inverting amplifier: <b>v₂·[R₃/(R₂+R₃)]·(1+R_f/R₁)</b>. With <b>R₁ = R₂</b> and <b>R₃ = R_f</b> the second collapses to <b>+v₂R_f/R₁</b>, so <b>v_out = (R_f/R₁)(v₂ − v₁)</b>. The ability to ignore what both inputs share is <b>common-mode rejection</b>, and it is what lets you read a millivolt sensor sitting on top of several volts of noise. It depends entirely on those resistor ratios matching: mismatch them by 1% and a large common-mode signal leaks through, which is why precision instrumentation uses matched resistor networks rather than four separate parts.'},
  {n:'A bridge amplifier — reading a sensor', ex:'a Wheatstone bridge into a difference amp', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibBridgeAmp({}), pane:'power', probe:[-4,0],
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Four resistors in a bridge, one of them 5% high — as a strain gauge or thermistor would be. The bridge output is a few tens of millivolts riding on 2.5 V of common mode; the difference amplifier rejects the 2.5 V and multiplies only the difference, by 47.',
   note:'A Wheatstone bridge is two dividers compared against each other. Balanced, <b>R_a/R_b = R_c/R_d</b>, its output is exactly zero — and a null is the easiest thing in the world to measure precisely, which is why bridges have been the basis of accurate measurement since 1833. Unbalance one arm by a fraction δ and the output is about <b>V·δ/4</b>: small, differential, and sitting on half the excitation voltage. That is precisely the signal a difference amplifier is built for. Put probe A on one midpoint and probe B on the other and the readout gives you the bridge output directly, along with the Thévenin resistance the amplifier is loading.'},
  {n:'A second-order active filter', ex:'Sallen–Key: two poles, no inductor', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibSallenKey({}), pane:'bode', drive:'V1', bode:{ f0:10, f1:1e6, n:190, probe:3 } },
   out:'Two resistors, two capacitors and a unity-gain buffer give a response that rolls off at 40 dB per decade instead of 20 — a second-order filter, with no inductor anywhere in it.',
   note:'Cascading two RC sections directly does not work: the second loads the first and the corner smears out. The op amp solves that by buffering — it presents an infinite input impedance to the first section and drives the second from a source impedance of nearly zero, so the two poles stay independent. The feedback from the output back into C1 then controls the damping, letting you place the poles anywhere you like: <b>Butterworth</b> for the flattest passband, <b>Bessel</b> for the cleanest step response, <b>Chebyshev</b> for the steepest cutoff at the price of ripple. Change C1 alone and watch the peak at the corner rise as the damping falls.'},
  {n:'Comparator and Schmitt trigger', ex:'positive feedback makes a decision stick', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibSchmitt({}), pane:'scope', tscale:2e-1,
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'The output can only ever be at one rail or the other. With positive feedback the switching threshold moves as soon as it switches, so the input must come back past a <i>different</i> level to switch it again — the trace shows the output flipping decisively, well away from where it flipped last.',
   note:'Without feedback a comparator on a slow or noisy input chatters: right at the threshold, a microvolt of noise flips it repeatedly. Feeding a fraction of the output <i>back to the + input</i> creates <b>hysteresis</b> — two thresholds, <b>±v_sat·R₁/(R₁+R_f)</b>, one to switch on and a lower one to switch off. Any noise smaller than the gap between them is now ignored completely. This is the same idea as a house thermostat that heats to 21 °C but does not restart until 19 °C, and for the same reason: to stop a decision oscillating.'},
  {n:'A relaxation oscillator — feedback that never settles', ex:'a Schmitt trigger charging its own capacitor', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckLibRelax({}), uic:true, pane:'scope', tscale:5e-2,
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'Nothing drives this circuit at all — no input source anywhere. It oscillates on its own, producing a square wave at the output and a triangle-ish exponential across the capacitor, because the state it is trying to reach is one it can never occupy.',
   note:'The capacitor charges towards whichever rail the output is at. As soon as it passes the Schmitt threshold the output flips, and now the capacitor is charging towards the <i>other</i> rail — so it turns round and heads back. There is no equilibrium, only a limit cycle, with period <b>T = 2RC·ln(1 + 2R₁/R_f)</b>. Two ingredients are needed and both are here: <b>positive feedback</b> to give two stable outputs, and a <b>delay</b> (the RC) to stop it choosing either. Every clock, blinker and beeper works this way.'}
]},
{ g:'Switches and sources', items:[
  {n:'Switching: charge, then discharge', ex:'the same τ, run twice', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'dc', val:5 },
                                     [{kind:'SW',name:'S1',mode:'time',ton:2e-3,toff:8e-3,period:16e-3},
                                      {kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:1e-6,ic:0}]),
          uic:true, pane:'scope', tscale:5e-3, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A timed switch closes at 2 ms and opens at 8 ms, over and over. The capacitor charges towards 5 V while it is closed and then holds its charge when the circuit is broken — with nowhere to discharge to, it simply stays where it was.',
   note:'Select S1 to change when it opens and closes, or set it to <b>manual</b> and flip it by hand while the simulation runs. Both kinds are real conductances — a closed switch is 10 mΩ and an open one is 1 GΩ, never literally zero or infinite, because an ideal switch would make the matrix singular the moment it opened onto a capacitor. There is also a <b>voltage-controlled switch</b> in the palette with a threshold and hysteresis, which is what a relay or a MOSFET driver behaves like.'},
  {n:'The voltage-controlled switch', ex:'a threshold with hysteresis — a relay, in effect', stage:'ckLab',
   /* the control pins are at x−1 and the contacts at x+1, so the deciding
      circuit is laid out on the left and the switched one on the right, with
      the two sharing only a ground return — as a relay does */
   opts:{ build: () => ckDemoSch({
            c: [['V','V1',-6,0,{rot:180, wave:'sin', amp:5, freq:200}],
                ['SWV','S1',0,0,{vth:2, vhys:0.5, ron:0.01}],
                ['V','V2',4,8,{rot:180, wave:'dc', val:5}],
                ['R','R1',10,8,{val:1000}],
                ['GND','G1',-2,-6]],
            w: [[-5,0, -5,1], [-5,1, -1,1],
                [-1,-1, -1,-6], [-7,0, -7,-6], [-7,-6, 14,-6],
                [5,8, 9,8], [11,8, 14,8], [14,8, 14,3], [14,3, 1,3], [1,3, 1,1],
                [1,-1, 1,-6],
                [3,8, 3,5], [3,5, -4,5], [-4,5, -4,-6]] }),
          pane:'scope', tscale:5e-2, show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'The control pins watch a 5 V sine; the contacts carry a separate 5 V circuit through a 1 kΩ load. The contacts close when the control passes +2.5 V and do not open again until it falls back below +1.5 V — a deliberate gap of 1 V.',
   note:'Two circuits that share no wire at all: one decides, the other carries the current. That is what a relay does, and what a MOSFET driven by a comparator does. The <b>hysteresis</b> is the important part — with a single threshold, a slow or noisy control signal makes the contacts chatter across it, and the switch would open and close hundreds of times per crossing. Two thresholds, one to close and a lower one to open, means any disturbance smaller than the gap is ignored completely. The contacts are real conductances, 10 mΩ closed and 1 GΩ open, because a literally infinite resistance would make the matrix singular the moment the switch opened onto a capacitor.'},
  {n:'Any waveform you can write down', ex:'v(t) = anything, typed as a formula', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'expr', expr:'5*exp(-t/0.004)*sin(tau*400*t) + 2*sign(sin(tau*50*t))' },
                                     [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:2e-7}]),
          pane:'scope', tscale:5e-2 },
   out:'This source is not one of the presets — it is the expression 5e^(−t/4ms)·sin(2π·400t) + 2·sign(sin(2π·50t)), typed in and compiled. A decaying ringing burst riding on a square wave, driving an RC low-pass that visibly rounds off its edges.',
   note:'Select V1 and edit the formula. Any function of <b>t</b> works, using the same parser the rest of the laboratory uses for fields: <b>sin cos tan asin acos atan sinh cosh tanh exp ln log sqrt abs sign floor min max atan2</b>, with <code>pi</code> and <code>tau</code>. That is enough for anything piecewise — <code>sign(sin(tau*f*t))</code> is a square wave, <code>2*(f*t - floor(f*t)) - 1</code> a sawtooth, <code>t*(t&lt;0.01)</code>-style gating via <code>min</code> and <code>max</code>. There are also thirteen ready-made waveforms in the dropdown, from DC and sine through pulse, chirp and band-limited noise.'},
  {n:'Dependent sources — the four of them', ex:'VCVS, VCCS, CCVS, CCCS', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckDemoSch({
            c: [['V','V1',-6,1,{rot:180, wave:'sin', amp:2, freq:500}],
                ['VCVS','E1',0,0,{gain:3}],
                ['R','R1',4,1,{val:1000}],
                ['GND','G1',-1,-4]],
            w: [[-5,1,-1,1], [-7,1,-7,-4], [-7,-4,-1,-4], [-1,-1,-1,-4],
                [1,1,3,1], [5,1,5,-4], [-1,-4,5,-4], [1,-1,1,-4]] }),
          pane:'scope', show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true } },
   out:'A voltage-controlled voltage source with µ = 3: whatever appears across its control pins comes out three times larger. The output is a copy of the input, scaled — and unlike a transformer it works at DC.',
   note:'Four dependent sources cover every linear device there is: <b>VCVS</b> (gain µ, volts to volts), <b>VCCS</b> (transconductance g_m, volts to amps — a valve or a FET), <b>CCVS</b> (transresistance r, amps to volts) and <b>CCCS</b> (current gain β, amps to amps — a bipolar transistor). They are how a transistor gets into a linear circuit model at all: you find the operating point, then replace the device with the dependent source that describes its slope there. The two current-controlled kinds need a branch to sense, so they take the name of a voltage source or inductor to watch. Place them from the palette and try each in turn.'}
]},
{ g:'The field behind the circuit', items:[
  {n:'What "voltage" actually is', ex:'V is a potential; E = −∇V is the field that pushes', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'dc', val:10 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:1000},{kind:'R',name:'R2',val:1000}]),
          pane:'none', probe:[3,2],
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true, heat:true, equipot:true, efield:true } },
   out:'The same divider as before, with the field switched on. Laplace\'s equation is solved in the plane of the board with every conductor held at the potential the circuit solve gave it, so the coloured map is the real potential landscape and the arrows are E = −∇V. Drag the probe anywhere to read both.',
   note:'Circuit theory is a summary of electromagnetism, not a replacement for it. "Voltage across R₁" means the line integral <b>∫E·dl</b> from one end to the other, and it is well defined only because an electrostatic field has <b>∇×E = 0</b> and therefore a potential. Look at what the solution shows: the wires are equipotentials (a good conductor cannot support a field along itself, or charge would keep accelerating), and essentially the whole potential drop happens across the resistor bodies — which is precisely why those and not the wires get hot. Inside the resistor the potential falls linearly, which is what uniform resistivity means. The field outside is not zero either: surface charges on the wires arrange themselves to produce exactly the field needed to push the current along, which is the part circuit diagrams never show you.'},
  {n:'The magnetic field of the currents', ex:'B = (µ₀/4π)∫I dl × r̂/r², out of the board', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'dc', val:10 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:2}]),
          pane:'none', probe:[5,3],
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true, bfield:true } },
   out:'A 5 A loop, and the magnetic field it makes. B points straight out of the board, so it is drawn as ⊙ where it comes toward you and ⊗ where it goes away — out of the page inside the loop and into it outside, exactly as the right-hand rule says. Drag the probe and the readout gives B in tesla.',
   note:'The electric field came from the potentials; this comes from the currents, and the circuit solve knows both exactly. Every conductor on the board is a straight segment carrying a known current, so <b>Biot–Savart</b> integrates along each one in closed form and the total is a superposition. Two things are worth checking against what you already know. Far from a long straight run the field approaches <b>B = µ₀I/2πd</b> — at 1 cm from 1 A that is 20 µT, comparable with the Earth\'s own 50 µT, and the readout gives the ratio. And the two sides of the return loop carry current in <i>opposite</i> directions, so their fields oppose and largely cancel outside the loop: that is precisely why signal wires are run as tight pairs and why a twisted pair radiates so little. Widen the loop by dragging a part and watch the external field grow.'},
  {n:'Inside a capacitor', ex:'the field between the plates, fringing and all', stage:'ckLab',
   opts:{ build: () => ckSeriesLoop({ wave:'sin', amp:5, freq:500 }, [{kind:'R',name:'R1',val:2000},{kind:'C',name:'C1',val:5e-7}]),
          pane:'scope', tscale:2e-2, probe:[9,0.6],
          show:{ volts:true, amps:true, labels:true, carriers:true, heat:true, equipot:true, efield:true } },
   out:'Watch the field between the plates build and collapse and reverse as the AC drives it, always in step with the voltage across them and always a quarter cycle out of step with the current. At the plate edges the field bulges outward — the fringing that makes a real capacitor slightly larger than the parallel-plate formula predicts.',
   note:'This is where the energy actually goes. The stored energy <b>U = ½Cv²</b> is not "in the capacitor" as an abstraction — it is in the field, at a density <b>u = ½ε₀E²</b> everywhere between and around the plates, and the readout tracks it as the field grows and dies. Notice too that no charge crosses the gap: the carriers pile onto one plate and leave the other, and the "current through the capacitor" is charge arriving and departing, not passing. Maxwell\'s displacement current <b>ε₀ ∂E/∂t</b> is what keeps Ampère\'s law consistent across that gap — and it is the same argument you can watch being made in the electromagnetism wing.'}
]}];

